{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` and\n`src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` both declare `trace_id` as\n`[\"string\",\"null\"]`, described as \"Langfuse trace id when tracing is enabled; null\notherwise\", but `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:259` and `:719` pass `trace_id=None`\nunconditionally. Nothing in rebar produces a per-run identity, so a verdict cannot name the\ntrace that produced it and no header can carry a run-stable value.\n\n## Approach\n\nMint one correlation identity per gate run at the two existing run boundaries, which already\nresolve the run's config exactly once: `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py:167`\n(plan-review, with `ctx.ticket_id` in scope) and `:762` (completion-verifier, with\n`ticket_id` in scope). Both open `gate_config(cfg)`, whose contextvar every op reads back\nthrough `resolve_gate_config` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:99-107`).\n\nAttach the identity by building a new config with `dataclasses.replace(cfg, ...)` before\n`gate_config(cfg)` rather than mutating the caller's object, so a caller-supplied config is\nnever modified in place.\n\nThe identity carries exactly three values: `trace_id` (32 lowercase hex characters from\n`secrets.token_hex(16)`), `ticket_id` (the run boundary's own `ticket_id`), and `operation`\n(the literal `\"plan_review\"` or `\"completion_verifier\"`, chosen by which boundary minted it).\nThere is deliberately no separate `run_id`: `trace_id` already identifies the run uniquely and\na second identifier would have no distinct consumer. The 32-hex lowercase shape matches the W3C trace-id\nformat so a later change can supply a real OpenTelemetry trace id into the same field without\na schema or format migration.\n\nRandom per run rather than derived from the ticket id: a derived value would merge every\nre-review of a ticket into one trace, making runs indistinguishable, and the verdict's\n`trace_id` field already provides the path back from a specific verdict to its run.\n\nThis value reaches pool workers unchanged because both plan-review pools submit through\n`generation._submit_ctx`, which is `ex.submit(contextvars.copy_context().run, fn, *args)`\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py:154-165`), used by `pass1.py:426-427` and\n`container_stage.py:260`.\n\nPopulate the verdict by reading `req.config.trace_id` at the existing `trace_id=` arguments in\n`src/rebar/llm/runner.py` (`:259` and `:719`), replacing the hardcoded `None`. The seam is\n`req.config`, not a runner instance attribute: the resolved gate config is handed to the op's\n`RunRequest` as `config=` (`src/rebar/llm/operations.py:209-212`), and `:259` sits inside\n`FakeRunner.run`, which holds no instance config and already reaches configuration only through\n`req.config` (`req.config.repo_path` at `:264`). Per-request config derivations such as\n`max_output_cfg` build with `dataclasses.replace`, so the identity fields ride through them\nunchanged.\n\nBecause the emitted meaning of `trace_id` changes — from \"a Langfuse trace id, present only\nwhen tracing is enabled\" to \"a rebar-minted per-run correlation id, present inside any gate\nrun\" — the two schema files that define that vocabulary are updated in the same change, so the\ndocumented contract never contradicts the emitted value.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the run-identity fields on `LLMConfig` and their accessor.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — mint the identity at both run boundaries.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — read `req.config.trace_id` instead of passing `None`.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` — restate the `trace_id` description.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` — restate the `trace_id` description.\n- `tests/unit/test_run_correlation.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests plus a schema-validation assertion. The mechanism is contextvar\npropagation and verdict field population, both of which run for real in-process; no\nsubprocess or network boundary is involved.\n\n- Happy path: within one `gate_config` scope every `resolve_gate_config()` read returns the\n  same `trace_id`, and a produced verdict carries it.\n- Edge path: two successive scopes produce different `trace_id` values, and the value is\n  `None` outside any gate run so standalone ops are unchanged.\n- Concurrency path: a value read from inside a `_submit_ctx`-submitted pool task equals the\n  value read on the submitting thread, exercising the real `copy_context()` seam rather than\n  asserting on it structurally.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Inside one gate run, `resolve_gate_config().trace_id` is identical across repeated\n      reads; checked by a test asserting equality across three reads in one `gate_config`\n      scope.\n- [ ] Two gate runs produce different `trace_id` values; checked by a test collecting the\n      value from two successive scopes and asserting inequality.\n- [ ] `trace_id` is 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters; checked by a test asserting\n      `re.fullmatch(r\"[0-9a-f]{32}\", value)`.\n- [ ] A value read inside a `_submit_ctx` pool task equals the submitting thread's value;\n      checked by a test submitting a reader through the real `_submit_ctx` and comparing.\n- [ ] A produced gate verdict carries the run's `trace_id` rather than `null`, and still\n      validates against `review_result.schema.json`; checked by a test asserting the field\n      and running the schema validator over the verdict.\n- [ ] Both schema files describe `trace_id` as a per-gate-run correlation id in W3C 32-hex\n      shape, present inside any gate run and null for standalone ops, with no residual claim\n      that it is a Langfuse trace id gated on tracing being enabled; checked by a grep\n      asserting \"Langfuse\" no longer appears in either `trace_id` description.\n- [ ] The identity survives a per-request config derivation; checked by a test asserting\n      `max_output_cfg(cfg).trace_id == cfg.trace_id`.\n- [ ] Outside any gate run the identity is `None` and verdicts are unchanged; checked by a\n      test asserting `trace_id is None` for a standalone op."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787706499883446001,"uuid":"de7a28f8-87c5-4207-80b9-09e517f769d9"}